{"text":"MODEL_FEEDBACK_JSON:
{
  "positive_alignment_reasons": [
    "The capacitor assortment and transistor kit fit my breadboards, PCB supplies and relay module because they would let me build and experiment with more circuits.",
    "The alligator test leads felt especially useful because they make temporary connections easier when testing boards and power circuits. Magnet wire also relates to my ferrite cores, although I would need a winding project first."
  ],
  "negative_misalignment_reasons": [
    "Automatically flashing LEDs and a hundred identical 75-ohm resistors serve specific purposes that I have not shown a need for; being electronic components alone does not make them useful next purchases.",
    "Oscilloscope probes depend on equipment I have not shown that I own, while large LED packs may duplicate supplies from my earlier PCB kit. The component tester sounds relevant, but I do not yet have a regular troubleshooting need."
  ],
  "overgeneralized_signals": [
    "My electronics purchases seem to have been taken as interest in almost any electronics component or accessory, regardless of its particular function.",
    "Buying project supply kits seems to have been treated as a reason to suggest more bulk supplies, even when I may already have enough or only need a specific part."
  ],
  "missing_or_underweighted_signals": [
    "I am more likely to choose something that helps me use the boards and parts I already have, such as temporary test connections or an assortment of useful component values.",
    "Relevance does not always mean I would buy now: I need a concrete project, an unmet supply need or confirmed equipment compatibility. My most recent purchase was caster wheels, so electronics may not be my current project."
  ],
  "summary_diagnosis": "The suggestions recognize my circuit-building interests, but give too little weight to immediate usefulness, existing supplies and whether a purchase requires a specific project or compatible equipment."
}"}
